WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.820
Leaf cutter ants farm and eat fungus in
underground nests.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:11.179
Their colonies harvest around 150 kilograms of plant
matter every year to feed their fungus crop.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:15.740
To understand how the ants turn
leaves into so much fungus food

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:19.040
scientists filmed their behavior in the
lab.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:22.840
They found that leaf cutter ants have prehensile leg tips

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.300
which they used to delicately grasp and manipulate leaves

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:29.130
as they cut them into tiny
fragments.

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:33.400
These dexterous tips are more flexible than non-prehensile ones--

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.420
allowing a precision grip.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:41.019
The team calculated that the ants must
cut nearly three kilometers of leaf edge to

00:00:41.019 --> 00:00:46.289
process one square meter of plant matter--
putting leaf cutter ants near the limit

00:00:46.289 --> 00:00:51.589
of their energy budget. But the ants
minimize their work by choosing smaller leaves.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.800
and feeding them to the fungus as fragments rather than pulp.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:01.240
The ants prehensile leg tips help them
harvest food for their fungus crop more efficiently,

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:06.440
giving them one leg up in
getting fungus from farm to table.

